@* One line in a list that is ticked: a box, what it is called, and whatever follows. Row's sibling
   rather than a mode of it - the two look identical and are not the same thing. Four pages wrote it out
   by hand, and the only differences between them were which of the trailing spans they added.

   Two shapes, because the words either open something or they do not:

   - Nothing to open: a <label>, so the whole line hands its press to the box inside it. A <button> may
     not sit inside a label at all, which is why the shape has to change rather than the title alone.
   - Something to open: a <div>, with the title as its own <button> beside the box. Then the box takes
     the tick and the words take the press, which is the one meaning a press has anywhere else in Orbit
     - see TaskListChecklist, whose grouped view has drawn its rows this way all along and whose flat
     view used to tick when the same words were pressed there.

   The press stops here either way. A tick row can sit inside something else that is pressable - see
   NoteSummary, whose whole card opens the editor - and ticking a line there means ticking that line,
   not opening the note to write in. Nothing is lost where there is nothing above to reach: the other
   three sit in plain cards. *@
@if (OnTitlePressed.HasDelegate)
{
    <div class="check-row @AdditionalClass" @onclick="() => { }" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
        <input type="checkbox" checked="@IsTicked" disabled="@IsDisabled" aria-label="@AriaLabel"
               @onchange="args => OnTicked.InvokeAsync((bool)args.Value!)" />
        <button type="button" class="check-row-text check-row-text-opens" @onclick="OnTitlePressed">@Title</button>
        @Trailing
    </div>
}
else
{
    <label class="check-row @AdditionalClass" @onclick="() => { }" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
        <input type="checkbox" checked="@IsTicked" disabled="@IsDisabled" aria-label="@AriaLabel"
               @onchange="args => OnTicked.InvokeAsync((bool)args.Value!)" />
        <span class="check-row-text">@Title</span>
        @Trailing
    </label>
}

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Title { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [Parameter] public bool IsTicked { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public bool IsDisabled { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What the box alone says, for a reader who never sees the words beside it. Left off where the
    /// title already reads as the whole question - a contact's name in a list of who to add to a group.
    /// Worth giving wherever <see cref="OnTitlePressed"/> is: that shape is not a label, so the box no
    /// longer takes its name from the words next to it and would otherwise have none.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string? AriaLabel { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Opening whatever this row names. Without one the row is a label and its words tick the box;
    /// with one the words are their own control and the box keeps the tick to itself.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnTitlePressed { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whatever follows the title - a category mark, a meta span, several of each. Row's own Trailing, and for the same reason: how many and which vary by row.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Trailing { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public EventCallback<bool> OnTicked { get; set; }

    /// <summary>"completed" where a ticked row is struck through, and whatever else the caller marks it with.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? AdditionalClass { get; set; }
}
